The shared mutable state problem.

Virtual threads and structured concurrency made running work in parallel cheap and safe. But the moment two of those threads touch the same piece of data, a new and much subtler kind of bug appears — one that doesn't crash, doesn't throw, and usually passes every test you write. This is the heart of the module's second half, and the reason locks, atomics, and the memory model exist at all. The enemy has a name: shared, mutable state.

Start from what's safe. Every thread gets its own call stack, so local variables and method parameters are private to that thread. An integer total declared inside a method is never a concurrency problem. The danger is objects on the heap — the fields of a shared object, static fields, the elements of a shared collection. When two threads hold a reference to the same object and both write its fields, they are stepping on each other. A counter with a count field is perfectly fine with one thread. Hand the same counter to two threads, and it quietly breaks.

Here's why. The trap is that count plus plus looks atomic, but it isn't. The java virtual machine turns it into three separate steps. First, read the current count — say it's forty-one. Second, add one, computing forty-two. Third, write forty-two back. Now picture two threads interleaving between those steps. Thread A reads forty-one. Before it writes, thread B also reads forty-one. Both compute forty-two. Both write forty-two. Two increments happened, but the count only went up by one. That's a lost update — and it's why a counter hammered by two threads ends up below the number of increments. Ask two threads to each increment one hundred thousand times, and you'd expect two hundred thousand. Instead you get some smaller number, and a different one every run.

That is exactly what a race condition is: the result depends on the interleaving of threads — on who happens to run when. Nothing is deterministic. The same code gives a different answer each run, because the java virtual machine, the operating system scheduler, and the hardware are all free to interleave however they like. And read-modify-write, like count plus plus, is only the obvious form. Even a plain shared boolean flag can be read stale by another thread, because there's no guarantee that a write on one thread is ever seen by another. That visibility half of the problem is what volatile and the memory model, later in this module, address.

The cruel part is that this bug hides. With one thread, or under light load, or on your laptop, the bad interleaving may almost never happen — the test passes, the demo works, it ships. Then production runs it on more cores under real contention, and the counts drift, the cache corrupts, the balance goes negative. A race condition is a latent bug: present in the code, absent from most runs. That's what makes it dangerous.

So what do we do about it? There are only two fundamental cures, and everything ahead is a variation on one of them. The first is: don't share. Give each thread its own data — that's thread confinement — or make the shared data immutable, so there's nothing to write. A record, a final field, an unmodifiable collection. The safest concurrency is no mutation at all. The second cure is: when threads genuinely must share mutable state, coordinate every access, so the read-modify-write can't be split. A lock — synchronized, or a reentrant lock — makes those three steps one indivisible unit. And atomic variables do the same thing in hardware for a single value. The next sections build exactly those tools. But the mindset to carry in is this: shared, mutable, and concurrent — pick at most two.
